---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas102.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Equity-Settled — Recognition & Grant Date Fair Value" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Vesting Conditions — Market vs Non-Market" },
        { id: "s5", label: "SEBI (SBEB) Regulations Interaction" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Cash-Settled Share-based Payment" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Modifications, Cancellations & Forfeitures" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Group Share-based Payment Arrangements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 102 vs IFRS 2 — Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The same expense-recognition principle as IFRS 2</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 102 applies the IDENTICAL Conceptual Framework logic as IFRS 2 — receiving employee SERVICE in exchange for share options is genuinely no different from paying cash wages, and must be recognised as an EXPENSE as those services are consumed. India's convergence approach means this fundamental principle is carried forward WITHOUT modification.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 102 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Expense recognition</strong> — services received in exchange for equity instruments are expensed as consumed, matching employee service to the vesting period — IDENTICAL to IFRS 2</span>
          <span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> — omitting ESOP expense would understate an Indian company's true remuneration cost, just as under IFRS 2 globally</span>
          <span><strong>Interaction with Indian securities regulation</strong> — for LISTED Indian companies, Ind AS 102's accounting sits ALONGSIDE (not instead of) SEBI's own regulatory framework governing ESOP scheme APPROVAL and administration (Section 5) — a genuinely India-specific regulatory layer that IFRS 2 has no direct equivalent for</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Ind AS 102 is one of the MOST closely converged Ind AS standards relative to its IFRS counterpart — the accounting MECHANICS are essentially identical; the main practical differences relate to INDIAN REGULATORY interaction (SEBI) and TAX treatment context, not the core accounting principles themselves.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles — identical to IFRS 2</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-13-1"><span>Classification</span><span>Measurement basis</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Equity-settled</span><span>Fair value AT GRANT DATE, fixed — IDENTICAL to IFRS 2</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-13-1"><span>Cash-settled</span><span>Fair value REMEASURED at each reporting date — IDENTICAL to IFRS 2</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">ℹ️ There is NO substantive measurement difference between Ind AS 102 and IFRS 2 — this is genuinely a "near-verbatim" converged standard.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 102 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 102 applies to ALL share-based payment transactions — equity-settled, cash-settled, and choice-of-settlement arrangements — with EXCLUSIONS mirroring IFRS 2's own (business combination consideration under Ind AS 103, certain IAS 41/Ind AS 2 contribution scenarios).</div>
        <div class="note">In the Indian context, Employee Stock Option Plans (ESOPs), Employee Stock Purchase Plans (ESPPs), and Stock Appreciation Rights (SARs) are the most common instruments falling within Ind AS 102's scope, particularly for listed and startup companies.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Grant date</div><div class="glossary-def">The date the entity and counterparty agree to a share-based payment arrangement — IDENTICAL definition to IFRS 2.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">SEBI (SBEB) Regulations</div><div class="glossary-def">The Securities and Exchange Board of India's Share Based Employee Benefits Regulations, governing the APPROVAL and administration of ESOP schemes for listed Indian companies — a regulatory layer alongside, not replacing, Ind AS 102's accounting requirements.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Perquisite (Indian tax context)</div><div class="glossary-def">The Indian Income Tax Act term for the taxable benefit an employee receives on exercising ESOPs — a tax concept operating ALONGSIDE, and independently of, Ind AS 102's accounting expense recognition.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — EQUITY-SETTLED (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Equity-Settled — Recognition &amp; Grant Date Fair Value (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Identical mechanics to IFRS 2</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Total expense = Number of instruments expected to vest × Grant date fair value per instrument, recognised STRAIGHT-LINE over the vesting period, credited to equity</div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: An Indian tech startup's ESOP grant</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An Indian startup grants 50,000 options to 20 employees on 1 April 2023, conditional on 3 years' continued service, with a grant date fair value of ₹80 per option (using the Black-Scholes model, since the company is unlisted and has no market price). At the end of Year 1, the entity estimates 90% of employees will remain employed through vesting.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Year 1 expense</strong> = 50,000 × 90% × ₹80 × (1/3) = <strong>₹12,00,000</strong>, recognised as an employee benefit expense with a corresponding credit to the Employee Stock Options Outstanding reserve within equity — mechanically IDENTICAL to how an equivalent grant would be accounted for under IFRS 2.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — VESTING CONDITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Vesting Conditions — Market vs Non-Market</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Identical treatment to IFRS 2</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Service and non-market performance conditions affect the NUMBER of instruments expected to vest (re-estimated each period); market conditions are built into GRANT DATE FAIR VALUE and NEVER reversed even if the market condition is not achieved — this distinction, and its accounting consequences, are IDENTICAL between Ind AS 102 and IFRS 2, with no substantive divergence.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — SEBI (SBEB) REGULATIONS (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>SEBI (SBEB) Regulations Interaction (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely India-specific regulatory layer</div>
        <div class="principle">For LISTED Indian companies, ESOP schemes must comply with SEBI's Share Based Employee Benefits Regulations — governing shareholder APPROVAL requirements, minimum vesting periods, disclosure in the Directors' Report, and administration through an ESOP trust in certain structures. This regulatory framework operates ALONGSIDE Ind AS 102's accounting requirements — SEBI governs the SCHEME's legal/governance validity, while Ind AS 102 governs its ACCOUNTING TREATMENT once the scheme exists.</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: SEBI vs Ind AS 102 — two distinct requirements</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A listed Indian company's ESOP scheme requires shareholder approval via special resolution under SEBI SBEB Regulations before options can be granted. Once approved and granted, options carry a grant date fair value of ₹120 each.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">SEBI's regulations govern WHETHER and HOW the scheme could be legally implemented (shareholder approval process) — a GOVERNANCE requirement. Ind AS 102 THEN governs the ACCOUNTING once the scheme is validly implemented (fair value measurement, expense recognition over the vesting period) — the two frameworks operate on ENTIRELY different dimensions of the same underlying transaction, with NO direct IFRS 2 equivalent for the SEBI layer specifically.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — CASH-SETTLED ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Cash-Settled Share-based Payment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Identical mechanics to IFRS 2</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">For cash-settled arrangements (e.g. Stock Appreciation Rights, common in Indian unlisted/startup contexts where equity dilution is less desirable), the liability is measured at fair value and REMEASURED each period until settlement, with changes recognised in profit or loss — mechanically IDENTICAL to IFRS 2's own cash-settled treatment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — MODIFICATIONS, CANCELLATIONS & FORFEITURES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Modifications, Cancellations &amp; Forfeitures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Identical treatment to IFRS 2</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Modifications increasing fair value are recognised as incremental expense; decreases are ignored. Cancellation accelerates remaining expense immediately; forfeiture (for failing a service/non-market condition) reverses cumulative expense already recognised — NO substantive difference from IFRS 2's own treatment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — GROUP ARRANGEMENTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Group Share-based Payment Arrangements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Identical treatment to IFRS 2 — relevant for Indian subsidiaries of foreign multinationals</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where a FOREIGN parent grants its own equity instruments to employees of its INDIAN subsidiary, the Indian subsidiary recognises the services received as an expense (with a corresponding capital contribution credit), while the parent recognises the increase in its own equity — a scenario COMMONLY arising for Indian subsidiaries of US/European multinational groups, applying the SAME mechanics as IFRS 2.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures — largely identical to IFRS 2, plus Companies Act cross-references</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Description of each arrangement, vesting requirements, maximum term, method of settlement</span>
          <span>Number/weighted average exercise price of options — movements during the period</span>
          <span>Option pricing model inputs used to determine fair value</span>
          <span>Total expense recognised in the period</span>
          <span>Indian listed companies ALSO cross-reference SEBI SBEB Regulations disclosure requirements in the Directors' Report, a requirement operating alongside (not replacing) Ind AS 102's own note disclosures</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Classifying market vs non-market conditions — identical to IFRS 2</span>
              <span>Determining whether an Indian subsidiary's grant from a foreign parent creates a capital contribution</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Fair value using option pricing models, particularly for UNLISTED Indian companies (a common scenario for startups) where no market price exists, making volatility estimation genuinely challenging</span>
              <span>Estimated forfeiture rates</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">⚠️ For UNLISTED Indian companies (a very common scenario given India's large startup ecosystem), estimating VOLATILITY for option pricing models is particularly challenging given the absence of an observable market price — often requiring comparable listed company data as a proxy.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IND AS 102 vs IFRS 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 102 vs IFRS 2 — Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS 2</span><span>Ind AS 102</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Core recognition/measurement principles</span><span>Grant date fair value (equity-settled); remeasured fair value (cash-settled)</span><span>IDENTICAL — no substantive divergence</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Market vs non-market conditions</span><span>Built into fair value vs affecting vesting number</span><span>IDENTICAL</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Regulatory approval layer for listed companies</span><span>No direct global equivalent — governed by local securities regulators where applicable</span><span>SEBI's Share Based Employee Benefits Regulations govern scheme approval/administration for LISTED Indian companies — a genuinely India-specific regulatory overlay</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Tax treatment interaction</span><span>Varies by jurisdiction, generally separate from accounting</span><span>Indian "perquisite" taxation under the Income Tax Act operates INDEPENDENTLY of Ind AS 102's accounting expense — a distinct but parallel Indian regulatory dimension</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Terminology</span><span>"Share-based payment," "equity instruments"</span><span>Same terminology, though Indian practice commonly uses "ESOP" (Employee Stock Option Plan) as the practical shorthand</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Ind AS 102 is genuinely one of the MOST closely converged Ind AS standards — the accounting SUBSTANCE is essentially identical to IFRS 2; the practical Indian-specific considerations relate almost entirely to REGULATORY (SEBI) and TAX (Income Tax Act) layers operating ALONGSIDE the accounting standard, not to the accounting itself.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Equity-settled expense recognised each period</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Credited to ESOP Reserve within equity</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Forfeiture (service/non-market condition not met)</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Cumulative expense reversed</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Exercise of vested options</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>ESOP Reserve transferred to share capital/securities premium</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Cash-settled (SAR) expense recognised each period</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Liability remeasured to fair value each period</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>SEBI SBEB scheme disclosures</span><span><span class="badge bp">Directors' Report</span></span><span>Alongside, not replacing, Ind AS 102 note disclosures</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 2</span><span>The GLOBAL counterpart standard, near-identical in substance</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 33</span><span>ESOPs are potential ordinary shares, tested for dilution in diluted EPS</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 24</span><span>ESOP grants to KMP require related party disclosure</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Companies Act, 2013 / SEBI SBEB Regulations</span><span>Governs the legal/governance validity and approval process for ESOP schemes at listed and certain unlisted Indian companies</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">ESOP</div><div class="glossary-def">Employee Stock Option Plan — the common Indian practical term for equity-settled share-based payment arrangements.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">ESOP Trust</div><div class="glossary-def">A trust structure some Indian listed companies use to administer their ESOP schemes, subject to SEBI SBEB Regulations, holding shares on behalf of the scheme.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Grant date", definition: "The date the entity and counterparty agree to a share-based payment arrangement — identical definition to IFRS 2." },
      { term: "SEBI (SBEB) Regulations", definition: "The Securities and Exchange Board of India's Share Based Employee Benefits Regulations, governing the approval and administration of ESOP schemes for listed Indian companies — a regulatory layer alongside, not replacing, Ind AS 102's accounting requirements." },
      { term: "Perquisite (Indian tax context)", definition: "The Indian Income Tax Act term for the taxable benefit an employee receives on exercising ESOPs — a tax concept operating alongside, and independently of, Ind AS 102's accounting expense recognition." },
      { term: "ESOP", definition: "Employee Stock Option Plan — the common Indian practical term for equity-settled share-based payment arrangements." },
      { term: "ESOP Trust", definition: "A trust structure some Indian listed companies use to administer their ESOP schemes, subject to SEBI SBEB Regulations, holding shares on behalf of the scheme." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
